#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147317 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29e7ead-5eae-5769-9502-7953d7018782" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "bd1d2ef2-9472-5e57-b071-fbbaf80023da" :question/math-academy-id "e-6731" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t > 0$. Determine whether each equation is forced or free, and whether it is damped.\n\n1. $y''+y'+3y = t$\n2. $y''+ 4y = e^t$\n3. $y''+y'+3y = 0$" :question/worked-solution "The differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = f(t)\n$$\n\nis forced if $f(t)$ is not identical to zero, and damped if $b>0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's classify each equation.\n\n- Equation I represents forced harmonic motion with damping. The forcing function $f(t)=t$ is not always zero, and there is a damping term with $b=1>0$.\n- Equation II represents forced harmonic motion without damping. The forcing function $f(t)=e^t$ is not always zero, but there is no damping term since $b=0$.\n- Equation III represents free (non-forced) harmonic motion with damping. The forcing function $f(t)=0$ is always zero, and there is a damping term with $b=1>0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147318 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "11a44451-5b9f-5892-b321-925b2a393551" :question/math-academy-id "e-6732" :question/problem "A particle of mass $3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 6\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2$. The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s}$, and an external force given by $F(t)=3t^2\\,\\textrm{N}$.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y + F(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$, $k$, $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3 y'' =-9y' -6 y+3t^2\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\ny'' + 3y'+2y = t^2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147319 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "aa77f4ef-67b6-5041-9a3b-1a36ffd5582d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6733" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$y''+2y' + 5 y = 50t$.\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)$?\n\n1. $y =Ae^{-t}+Be^{-2t}+25t+5$\n2. $y = R e ^ {-t} \\cos \\left(2t + \\phi \\right) +10t - 4$\n3. $y =e^{-t}\\left(A\\cos{2t} + B\\sin{2t}\\right)+10 t-4$\n\nNote that $A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t) = y_c(t)+y_p(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution $y_c(t)$, which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\ny''+2y' + 5 y = 0\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 2λ + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} + 2λ + 1) - 1 + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)^{2} + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)^{2} &= -4 \\\\\nλ + 1 &= ± 2\\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= -1 ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function $y_c(t)$ is can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{c}(t) &= e^{-t}(A\\cos 2t + B\\sin 2t), \\\\\ny_{c}(t) &= Re^{-t}\\cos (2t + ϕ), \\\\\ny_{c}(t) &= Re^{-t}\\sin (2t + ϕ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution $y_p(t)$. Note that the external force\n\n$$\nf(t)=50t\n$$\n\nis a linear function. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a linear function,\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha = 10$ and $\\beta = -4$. So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t)= 10 t - 4\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= e^{-t}(A\\cos 2t + B\\sin 2t) + 10t - 4, \\\\\ny(t) &= Re^{-t}\\cos (2t + ϕ) + 10t - 4, \\\\\ny(t) &= Re^{-t}\\sin (2t + ϕ) + 10t - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly II and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147320 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5266e491-2718-56a9-b5a7-f4a86a8a01c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position of a Particle Oscillating With Forced Harmonic Motion at a Moment"}] :question/id #uuid "20b53dff-43b9-5256-ab7d-67aff56db408" :question/math-academy-id "e-6734" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + y =2e^{-t}$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\pi$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "This is an initial value problem. Since the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and $y'(0)=0$. So, we want to solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + y =2e^{-t}, \\quad y(0)=0, \\quad y'(0)=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the general solution of the differential equation. The complementary solution $y_c(t)$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 1\n$$\n\nso we have\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{t} + B\\sin{t}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution $y_p(t)$. Note that the external force\n\n$$\nf(t) = 2e^{-t}\n$$\n\nis an exponential function, and the constant factor in the exponent $(-1)$ is not a root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular exponential solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{-t}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nSubstituting $y_p$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha=1$. Therefore, the particular solution $y_p(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) =e^{-t}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\cos{t} + B\\sin{t} + e^{-t}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. Substituting $y(0)=0$ gives $A=-1$, and differentiating $y$ and substituting $y'(0)=0$ gives $B=1$.\n\nSo, our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=-\\cos t + \\sin{t} +e^{-t}\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\pi$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\pi$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(π) &= -\\cos π + \\sin π + e^{- π} \\\\\n&= (1 + e^{- π})m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29e7ead-5eae-5769-9502-7953d7018782" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9401ffb9-8bd9-56c9-b878-fe376d9c18db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 3y^{′} = 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146720} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4d0c11-940f-5c5b-9148-66eeff98329d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} - 9y = 5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146721} {:answer/id #uuid "05245b84-0c54-55d6-abc7-e5b77437f286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 4y = \\frac{2}{t^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146722} {:answer/id #uuid "23589b8a-9051-5678-b3c9-3617b203e24d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 2y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146723} {:answer/id #uuid "6e844600-def7-5a16-aeca-e48c346e8a66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 4y^{′} + 2y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05245b84-0c54-55d6-abc7-e5b77437f286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 4y = \\frac{2}{t^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146722} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bde8bbf-2d44-5dab-9977-c4e241c9a60a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146719}] :question/id #uuid "c679c2bb-06e7-59ad-91c0-dfee5554ce16" :question/math-academy-id "q-64553" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t > 0$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion with damping?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c878b75d-e932-55a2-83a7-aabd31abfb4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146726} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f8a420-772c-571a-89b0-faca36abe05a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146727} {:answer/id #uuid "f799a0db-1498-5146-911f-9b13cf114596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146728} {:answer/id #uuid "7647f177-bde1-5aa4-9fe7-135fd62ea5e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146729} {:answer/id #uuid "781b9ee2-11d5-5e70-995e-7eb63f3b5a88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c878b75d-e932-55a2-83a7-aabd31abfb4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146726} :answer-field/id #uuid "b77713a1-0222-5166-8870-3fc508801954" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146725}] :question/id #uuid "31bc7182-8785-59ea-9a66-360f3942567f" :question/math-academy-id "q-64554" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 9y = 10e^{-t}$.\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)$?\n\n1. $y = R\\cos (3t + ϕ) + e^{-t}$\n2. $y = R\\sin (t + ϕ) + e^{-2t}$\n3. $y = A\\cos 3t + B\\sin 3t + e^{-t}$\n\nNote that $A$, $B$, $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdee774f-e5da-5f12-826a-dd95816847b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 15y = 6\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146732} {:answer/id #uuid "de65f081-e41b-51f9-bfc5-dadc4d88895f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 5y = 9\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146733} {:answer/id #uuid "1aeb4e3a-503c-50e5-9992-64a688ade267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 5y = 3\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146734} {:answer/id #uuid "2126cd8b-834b-5709-ba61-ab3d0b924c44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 15y = 3\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146735} {:answer/id #uuid "5017e889-e4c5-5292-9465-f5d650171618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 15y = 9\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1aeb4e3a-503c-50e5-9992-64a688ade267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 5y = 3\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146734} :answer-field/id #uuid "0480d242-9172-5eae-855a-3bce3e2200f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146731}] :question/id #uuid "9df2fe93-bc75-52a9-b88c-221b0f80d0b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-64555" :question/problem "A particle of mass $3kg$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 15kg/s^{2}$.\nAn external force $F(t) = 9\\sin (t)N$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5266e491-2718-56a9-b5a7-f4a86a8a01c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position of a Particle Oscillating With Forced Harmonic Motion at a Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbff926b-9464-55e4-bea0-0a95d63332b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1) = (\\sin 2 + 3)m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146762} {:answer/id #uuid "0045288f-c559-5b17-a27e-67f2a3b6f87d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1) = (\\cos 1 - 2)m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146763} {:answer/id #uuid "053fa0a8-bee7-5495-a910-f3f146ca22b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1) = (2\\cos 3 + \\sin 3 - 1)m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146764} {:answer/id #uuid "4400bf40-eb70-5eb3-84be-0a0e332cc631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1) = (\\cos 2 + 1)m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146765} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba546f7-d7b6-5922-be6f-07995204d489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1) = (\\cos 1 + \\sin 1 + 3)m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4400bf40-eb70-5eb3-84be-0a0e332cc631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1) = (\\cos 2 + 1)m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146765} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e4eb606-49f5-5bde-8a87-5a33db5fb64e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146761}] :question/id #uuid "9d1bc0c7-99f9-59d0-919b-05c0a19b6daa" :question/math-academy-id "q-64572" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 4y = 8t^{2}$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $1$ second."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5266e491-2718-56a9-b5a7-f4a86a8a01c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position of a Particle Oscillating With Forced Harmonic Motion at a Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8da1cad9-6868-5bc2-b70b-af02a231e953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(π) = (1 + 3e^{- π})in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146804} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4b7cb0-4c6b-55ae-b9a4-dd7484e32845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(π) = (3 - e^{-2π})in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146805} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb485fc-8b64-5588-a859-6a0fa240c800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(π) = 2(1 + 2e^{-2π})in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146806} {:answer/id #uuid "303fd9d8-cc54-5689-84a8-4affddc85cad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(π) = (4 - e^{- π})in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146807} {:answer/id #uuid "6943743f-b7d7-5c33-a4f9-c7c350fb88a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(π) = 2(1 - e^{- π})in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6943743f-b7d7-5c33-a4f9-c7c350fb88a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(π) = 2(1 - e^{- π})in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146808} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5dd9e9a-024f-5593-bd5c-1aaef00d52ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146803}] :question/id #uuid "1c4359cc-de3d-594a-82e3-7614419ad340" :question/math-academy-id "q-64646" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$, measured in inches, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion with light damping is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 5y = 34\\cos 2t$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $π$ seconds."}]}
